1
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:04,230
弗里曼：地球充满生机
(Freeman) Earth teems with life.

2
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:11,940
但是几十亿年前
But billions of years ago,

3
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,670
我们的星球只是一团 炽热的岩浆
our planet was just a ball of molten rock.

4
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,670
第一个地球人是从
Did the first Earthlings rise

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:21,040
一池塘冒泡的化学浓汤中 缓缓起身的吗？
from a chemical soup bubbling in a primordial pond?

6
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:24,580
或者，生命之种是从外太空飘来
Or did the seeds of life crash down from outer space?

7
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,920
现在，至少 科学近乎解决这个
Now,at last,science may be on the cusp

8
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:32,190
最永恒的奥秘——
of solving that most enduring mystery --

9
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:33,920
我们从何而来?
How did we get here?

10
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:45,470
空间，时间，生命本身
Space,time,life itself.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,580
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

12
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:55,410
♪穿越虫洞 第1x05集♪
♪Through the Wormhole 1x05♪

13
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:59,680
我们从何而来？
How Did We Get Here?

14
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:03,750
生命充满神秘
Life is full of mysteries,

15
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:08,560
但最吸引人的神秘是生命本身
but the most compelling mystery is life itself.

16
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:12,530
古希腊人相信 诸神用粘土造人
The ancient Greeks believed the gods shaped man from clay.

17
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:14,600
维京人传说存在两个大陆
The Vikings tell of two great continents,

18
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,230
一个由烈焰组成 一个由玄冰组成
one made of fire,one of ice.

19
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:20,800
当它们相遇，火花闪现
When they met,sparks flew,

20
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,710
第一个生命由此诞生
and the first living beings were born.

21
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:28,040
科学家们还在孜孜探求 这个老难题的答案
Scientists are still trying to solve this age-old conundrum --

22
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:29,880
化学家们可以通过简单操作
how simple chemicals

23
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:33,050
转化出生命分子
were somehow transformed into living molecules,

24
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:37,220
那些你我体内都具有的分子
molecules that eventually evolved into you and me.

25
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:42,330
但是，众里寻它千百度 答案可能就在灯火阑珊处
But the answers may at long last be close at hand,

26
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:47,360
而且要比我们所能想象的 更加令人吃惊
and more surprising than we could have ever imagined.

27
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:55,140
小时候，我经常 拿着放大镜东游西逛
As a kid,I used to go around with a magnifying glass,

28
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:57,070
试图用它生把火
trying to set things on fire.

29
00:01:57,070 --> 00:02:00,110
偶是破坏王？
Was I being destructive?

30
00:02:00,110 --> 00:02:02,450
我可不这么认为
I don't think so.

31
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:05,250
我是想看看能否用太阳的力量 创造点啥
I was seeing what I could create with the power of the sun.

32
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:09,450
对我来说 火花是具有某种魔力的
There was something magic to me about that spark.

33
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:17,230
在几十亿年前的地球上 也发生了这样的事吗？
Is this what happened on Earth billions of years ago?

34
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:19,700
是否有一个火花 将无生命的物质
Did a spark turn inanimate matter

35
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:23,600
转化为能够 生长，繁衍，演化的物质
into something that can grow,reproduce,and evolve --

36
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:27,400
转化为我们所定义的—生命?
something we would define as alive?

37
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:28,640
这是个谜
It's a puzzle

38
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:31,410
科学已经努力了很长时间 来完成这幅拼图
that science has been struggling to piece together.

39
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:34,010
Davies:我们有一个很棒的 生命进化理论
(Davies) We have a very good theory of life's evolution,

40
00:02:34,010 --> 00:02:36,850
但我们对生命起源却 从未达成共识
but we have no agreed theory of life's origin.

41
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:39,820
我们不知道 一堆无生命的化合物
We don't know how a mix of nonliving chemicals

42
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:41,480
是如何混在一起 转化为生命的
turns itself into a living thing.

43
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:43,420
Sutherland:那是个很好的问题
(Sutherland) That's a very good question.

44
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:45,690
因为还没有人能够完成
Because no one's actually experimentally converted

45
00:02:45,690 --> 00:02:47,660
将无生命转化为生命的实验
the nonliving into living,

46
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:49,830
我们不知道究竟需要怎么做
we don't know precisely what we need.

47
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:54,230
弗里曼：这也许会是一个 永远没有答案的谜团
(Freeman) It's a question that may never find an answer.

48
00:02:54,230 --> 00:02:58,840
但是科学家勇于探索 我们被创造的时刻
But the scientists who dare to probe our moment of creation

49
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:01,900
引领我们进入迷人的旅程
are leading us on a fascinating journey

50
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:04,640
通向出乎意料的目的地
to an unexpected destination.

51
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:11,680
为了揭开我们基因的秘密
To solve the mystery of our genesis,

52
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:14,450
我们需要回溯进化过程
we have to rewind evolution,

53
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:16,850
回到第一个生命诞生的
go back to the time and the place

54
00:03:16,850 --> 00:03:19,220
时间和地点
where the first living things came to be...

55
00:03:22,090 --> 00:03:26,860
40多亿年前，我们的太阳系
...Our solar system more than 4 billion years ago.

56
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:31,870
那时的太阳系可是和今天 大不同啊大不同
It was a very different place from the solar system we know.

57
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:35,570
太阳是一个年轻的恒星 比今天要凉一些
The sun was a young star,cooler than it is today.

58
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:38,010
地球却要热的多
Earth was much hotter,

59
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:42,550
刚刚开始从一团融化岩浆 渐渐固化
having just solidified from a molten ball of hot rock.

60
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:46,250
彗星和大陨石围绕着它
Comets and large meteorites were whirling all around it.

61
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:52,120
毁灭性的剧烈冲撞频繁发生
Fiery impacts were frequent and often devastating.

62
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,260
在地球的地质学史上
It's a period of earth's geological history

63
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:59,660
这段时期被称为"冥古代" 地狱般的时期
called the "Hadean," the age of hell.

64
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:07,100
地质学家Stephen Mojzsis 来自科罗拉多大学
Geologist Stephen Mojzsis,from the University of Colorado,

65
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,310
正在做回到冥古代的时间旅行
is traveling back in time to the Hadean,

66
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:13,310
试图寻找生命存在的证据
trying to discover evidence of life there.

67
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:16,510
将大约40亿年前的 地球环境可视化
It's interesting to visualize what you might see

68
00:04:16,510 --> 00:04:19,220
让我们看看站在冥古代的 地球表面会看到何种景象
standing on the surface of the Hadean Earth

69
00:04:19,220 --> 00:04:20,820
这真是个有意思的工作
some 4 billion years ago.

70
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:23,350
月亮会占满天空
The moon would fill the sky

71
00:04:23,350 --> 00:04:26,190
因为当时的月亮 距离地球近的多
because it was much closer to the Earth at that time.

72
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:28,660
而天空本身也怪模怪样
But the sky itself would appear different.

73
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:32,330
当时并不是美丽的蓝天
So rather than a beautiful sky blue,

74
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:34,830
而是红色的天
it must have been red

75
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:38,770
流星和彗星在天际划过
and blasted by meteors and comets.

76
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:44,140
弗里曼：冥古代的海洋也与今日
(Freeman) The oceans would also have looked different

77
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:45,640
面目不同
in the Hadean.

78
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:49,510
海水不是湛蓝清澈 而是暗绿色
They would not be blue and clear,but dark green,

79
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:51,880
充满了铁矿物质
filled with iron minerals.

80
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:55,080
在我们看来，这更像是一个 陌生的星球
To us,it would appear as an alien planet,

81
00:04:55,090 --> 00:04:58,220
不太可能维持生命的存在
incapable of sustaining life.

82
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:00,260
但是Stephen试图证明
But Stephen is convinced

83
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:02,390
这里已经有了原始的生命
it could have harbored primitive life,

84
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:05,160
就像在今天最极端的环境中
just like today's most extreme environments,

85
00:05:05,160 --> 00:05:10,400
仍有微生物能够找到生存之道
where microscopic organisms find a way to survive.

86
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,240
我们知道，看看我们今天的星球
We know,looking at our planet today --

87
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:17,540
无论是在最干燥的沙漠 最冷的冰川
whether in the driest desert,the coldest glacier,

88
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:20,480
最深的海洋 最高的山巅
the deepest ocean,the tallest mountain --

89
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:21,840
都存在生命
life exists.

90
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:25,050
我们怎么能肯定早期的地球 是个例外呢？
Why should we expect otherwise for the earliest earth?

91
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:28,080
弗里曼：古生物学家在岩石中寻找
(Freeman) Paleontologists look for evidence

92
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:31,290
古老生命留下的痕迹
of ancient life-forms fren in rocks.

93
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:33,790
目前，找到了5亿年前的
There are fossils of sea creatures and plants

94
00:05:33,790 --> 00:05:35,520
海生动物和植物化石
going back half a billion years.

95
00:05:35,530 --> 00:05:38,490
但是，更早存在的
But the more primitive,microscopic life

96
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,230
更加原始的微生物痕迹
that existed before that

97
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:42,130
更难探寻
is much harder to detect,

98
00:05:42,130 --> 00:05:44,900
而且，寻找冥古代时期的岩石
and finding rocks that date back to the Hadean

99
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,540
就是一件几乎不可能的事
is next to impossible.

100
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:51,710
几乎所有地球初期的岩石 都没有了
Almost all of early earth is now gone,

101
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:55,040
它们葬身熔岩流和海底
buried under lava flows and oceans.

102
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:59,150
但是仍然有很少的 40亿年之久的岩层露头
But a few rare outcrops of 4-billion-year-old rock

103
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,320
残留下来
do remain.

104
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,690
Stephen在格陵兰腹地
Stephen tracked one down

105
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:06,920
追寻着这样一处古老岩层
in a remote region in the heart of Greenland.

106
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:11,630
传统观点认为，地球历史上最初5亿年
The traditional wisdom was that there could not be a record

107
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:14,900
不会留下什么记录
of the first half billion years of earth history.

108
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:18,530
但是，通过仔细的搜索
Instead,what we have found by careful searches is

109
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:22,000
我们发现，不仅存在 这段时期的记录
that not only is there a record from this time period,

110
00:06:22,010 --> 00:06:27,240
而且，这些记录告诉我们 当时是一个非常宜居的世界
but the record reveals to us an eminently habitable world.

111
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:30,510
弗里曼：Stephen在格陵兰 找到的岩石
(Freeman) The rocks Stephen found in Greenland

112
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:35,150
是来自38亿年前的一片海洋
came from an ocean that formed 3.8 billion years ago.

113
00:06:35,150 --> 00:06:37,950
岩石遍布黑点
They were peppered with a series of black dots.

114
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:41,120
那是古代碳成分留下的痕迹
They were lumps of ancient carbon,

115
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:44,560
它们有非常不同寻常之处
and there was something very unusual about them.

116
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:47,330
碳有2种形式——
Carbon comes in two forms --

117
00:06:47,330 --> 00:06:49,970
普通碳，被称作碳12
normal carbon,called carbon 12,

118
00:06:49,970 --> 00:06:53,400
还有一种非常稀有的形式 称作碳13
and a much rarer form called carbon 13,

119
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:55,740
又称重碳
or heavy carbon.

120
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:58,040
通常的地质碳沉积中
Normal geological deposits of carbon

121
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:02,380
碳12和碳13存在一个精确比率
contain a precise ratio of carbon 12 to carbon 13,

122
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:04,410
但这些古老岩石上的碳痕迹 却不存在这种比率
but not these lumps.

123
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:06,820
生命会造成一些有趣的后果
Life does something interesting.

124
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:11,650
生命能够分辨出碳13
It discriminates against carbon 13.

125
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:16,190
所以生命体会造成
So the biological matter is profoundly enriched

126
00:07:16,190 --> 00:07:17,790
碳12的富集
in carbon 12.

127
00:07:17,790 --> 00:07:21,400
在这些世界上已知的 最古老岩石沉积物中
In the world's oldest known sedimentary rocks,

128
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:22,830
碳12的富集非常明显
it's very clear.

129
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:27,070
这是早期生命留下的
There's a carbon isotope signature

130
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:29,040
碳同位素特征
of early life.

131
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:38,110
弗里曼：Stephen无法确定 留下这种痕迹的
(Freeman) Stephen can't tell what the primitive life-form

132
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:41,720
原始生命形式究竟是什么样
that left this telltale signature was like,

133
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:46,020
但它一定能够在一个 严酷星球生存下来
but it must have been able to survive in a brutal environment,

134
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:51,030
这个星球被巨大的太空陨石 持续击打
a planet constantly pummeled by giant space rocks,

135
00:07:51,030 --> 00:07:54,030
大多数地质学家相信
a world where most geologists believe

136
00:07:54,030 --> 00:07:58,270
持续的冲击造成地球的 很大一部分融化
the deluge of impacts would have melted huge parts of earth,

137
00:07:58,270 --> 00:08:00,770
海洋也被煮干了
boiled its oceans dry,

138
00:08:00,770 --> 00:08:04,540
使整个星球成为不毛之地
and sterilized the entire planet.

139
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:08,440
然而Stephen Mojzsis相信
Yet Stephen Mojzsis is sure

140
00:08:08,450 --> 00:08:12,580
在这个地狱般的地球上 某处...
that somewhere on this hellish earth...

141
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:14,980
存在生命
There was life.

142
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:21,160
Stephen开发了一个电脑模拟程序
Stephen has developed a computer simulation

143
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:24,290
试图证明，40亿年前的冥古代
to prove our planet could have remained hospitable to life

144
00:08:24,290 --> 00:08:27,600
即使遭遇了剧烈的陨石轰炸
even during the intense bombing campaign it endured

145
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:32,070
我们的星球 仍在存在生命的庇护所
in the Hadean 4 billion years ago.

146
00:08:32,070 --> 00:08:35,770
这里的每一幕
Each of these episodes here

147
00:08:35,770 --> 00:08:39,680
都是在大轰炸时期尾声阶段
is an individual asteroid or comet

148
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:42,010
一次次独立的
coming into the earth

149
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:44,810
陨石或彗星撞击
at the time of the late heavy bombardment.

150
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:48,120
我们发现了撞击造成的 熔融池塘
We find that impact melt pools here --

151
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:50,150
熔岩湖
basically lava lakes,

152
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:54,720
有些熔岩湖的面积 和非洲大陆差不多
some of which are the size of the continent of Africa.

153
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:56,330
但是这些蓝色的区域
But this blue region here,

154
00:08:56,330 --> 00:08:58,590
也就是低温区域
which represents cold temperatures,

155
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:02,930
在这里，液态水仍然存在
are areas where liquid water is still stable.

156
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:06,240
即使是在早期太阳系的
Even in the intense bombardment epoch

157
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:08,470
大轰炸时代
of the early solar system,

158
00:09:08,470 --> 00:09:10,910
地球上依然有可能存在 一个适合生命的地方
the earth would have remained a habitable place.

159
00:09:10,910 --> 00:09:14,180
弗里曼：微生物藏身地下
(Freeman) Microbes deep down in the earth --

160
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:16,610
或者海洋深处
or deep in the ocean,

161
00:09:16,610 --> 00:09:19,980
类似于今天聚集在 火山通道的生物
like those that today gather around volcanic vents --

162
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:21,880
它们最有可能
would have the best chance

163
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:24,690
在这种毁灭性的爆炸中 生存下来
of surviving these devastating blows.

164
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:30,190
那些能够找到庇护所的生物体
What these bombardments do is favor organisms

165
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:32,800
可以经受住太空碎片的轰炸
that can find a sanctuary

166
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:37,270
挺过陨石的狂风暴雨
to ride out the raining storm of space debris

167
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:40,770
直到大轰炸时期的结束
until the epoch of bombardment is over

168
00:09:40,770 --> 00:09:43,710
然后，整个世界供其殖民了
and the whole world is left for them to colonize.

169
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:48,180
我们认为，大概38亿年前
And we think that happened on the Earth

170
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:52,880
大轰炸时期终于结束了
about 3.8 billion years ago,when the bombardment ceased.

171
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:00,590
弗里曼：Stephen的发现
(Freeman) Stephen's discovery

172
00:10:00,590 --> 00:10:02,830
也许可以确定生命起源的
may have pinpointed the time and place

173
00:10:02,830 --> 00:10:04,630
时间和地点
for the origin of life,

174
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:08,400
但并未告诉我们 生命是如何起源的
but it tells us nothing about how life actually started.

175
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,270
为了解决这个问题 科学家们必须在实验室中
To do that,scientists must re-create early earth

176
00:10:11,270 --> 00:10:12,640
重现早期地球
in their labs

177
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:17,510
试图能够看见那第一道 充满魔力的火花
and try to catch a glimpse of that first magical spark.

178
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:31,040
弗里曼：地球，45亿年前
(Freeman) Planet Earth,4 1/2 billion years ago.

179
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:33,110
如果我们踏上它的表面
If we stepped on its surface,

180
00:10:33,110 --> 00:10:36,980
融化的岩浆会立即 把我们烧成灰烬
molten lava would incinerate us immediately.

181
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:42,450
一口那时的空气足以 要了我们小命儿
One breath of its atmosphere would kill us.

182
00:10:42,450 --> 00:10:44,720
生命，如何能够在这样
How could life have formed

183
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:47,730
冒泡的，有毒的地狱中诞生？
in this bubbling,poisonous hell?

184
00:10:50,230 --> 00:10:52,800
1953年
In 1953,

185
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:56,630
两位无畏的化学家 试图解决这个问题
two intrepid chemists try to answer this question.

186
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:00,470
Stanley Miller和Harold Urey 设计了一个实验
Stanley Miller and Harold Urey designed an experiment

187
00:11:00,470 --> 00:11:03,570
模拟刚诞生的地球
to simulate our planet soon after its birth.

188
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:07,310
结果会是开创性的
The results would turn out to be so groundbreaking

189
00:11:07,310 --> 00:11:09,610
实验装置设在
that the apparatus has been preserved

190
00:11:09,610 --> 00:11:11,980
圣迭戈的斯克里普斯学院
at the Scripps Institution in San Diego

191
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:14,920
由他们从前的学生 Jeffrey Bada博士负责
by their former student Dr. Jeffrey Bada.

192
00:11:17,590 --> 00:11:18,860
乍看之下
At first glance,

193
00:11:18,860 --> 00:11:21,630
就像是一堆 各式各样的烧瓶和试管
it looks like just an assortment of flasks and tubes,

194
00:11:21,630 --> 00:11:23,760
但这是经过仔细设计的
but this was carefully designed to,

195
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:25,260
首先，一个烧瓶
first of all,have a flask

196
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:29,200
会呈现一个有蒸发作用的海洋
that would represent an evaporating ocean,

197
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,670
它和另一个烧瓶连接
and that was connected to a flask

198
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:33,840
这个烧瓶模拟大气
that represented the atmosphere.

199
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:37,310
在大气中，你可以看到电极
And in this atmosphere,you see these electrodes,

200
00:11:37,310 --> 00:11:41,350
你可以通过这些电极放电
and you can apply an electric discharge to these electrodes

201
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:43,610
模拟大气中的闪电
to simulate atmospheric lighting.

202
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:47,320
而相关产物 会通过这个冷凝器
And the products would condense out of the atmosphere

203
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:48,750
从大气中浓缩出来
via this condenser,

204
00:11:48,750 --> 00:11:50,820
进入这个试管
run into this tube,

205
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:52,990
回到这个烧瓶里
and then back into the water flask.

206
00:11:52,990 --> 00:11:56,360
弗里曼：也许是受 创世纪一书的启示
(Freeman) Perhaps inspired by the book of Genesis,

207
00:11:56,360 --> 00:12:00,100
Miller和Urey将他们生命起源的实验
Miller and Urey left their experiment on the origin of life

208
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:02,100
进行了7天
running for seven days.

209
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:06,040
然后，现在，模拟海洋的烧瓶
Then,as now,the flask representing the ocean

210
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:09,110
开始渐渐的变成暗棕色
slowly started to turn dark brown,

211
00:12:09,110 --> 00:12:12,940
充满了好像有毒污泥的东西
filling with a seemingly toxic sludge.

212
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:14,610
Dr. Bada:这是一个复杂的实验
(Dr. Bada) It is a tricky experiment.

213
00:12:14,610 --> 00:12:16,380
如果你不小心，可能会爆炸
If you're not careful,you can blow it up.

214
00:12:16,380 --> 00:12:18,850
而且，这个溶液毒性很高
And moreover,this solution is highly toxic.

215
00:12:18,850 --> 00:12:22,490
其中有大量的氢氰化物
It contains large amounts of hydrogen cyanide.

216
00:12:22,490 --> 00:12:24,860
你可永远不会想尝尝这个
You'd never want to try and drink this thing,

217
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:27,260
你会立刻死亡的
or you'd be dead in a second.

218
00:12:29,390 --> 00:12:33,560
弗里曼：但这些棕色糊糊中 有些不同寻常的东西
(Freeman) But this brown goo also contains something remarkable.

219
00:12:33,570 --> 00:12:37,870
在有毒的化合物中，还有氨基酸
Among the toxic chemicals are amino acids.

220
00:12:37,870 --> 00:12:42,340
氨基酸是构成蛋白质的基本材料
Amino acids are the basic building blocks of proteins,

221
00:12:42,340 --> 00:12:46,110
而生命，是由蛋白质构成的
and living things are built from proteins.

222
00:12:46,110 --> 00:12:50,550
蛋白质组成了 骨头，头发和皮肤
They make up bones,hair,and skin.

223
00:12:50,550 --> 00:12:52,480
这一个真正的突破
It was a major breakthrough.

224
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:53,850
截至目前
Up to this time,

225
00:12:53,850 --> 00:12:56,290
人们试图模拟早期大气
people had tried to make organic compounds

226
00:12:56,290 --> 00:12:59,920
制造有机化合物 但他们失败了
simulating an early atmosphere,but they'd always fail.

227
00:13:01,990 --> 00:13:05,260
弗里曼：但这只是 通向生命的一小步
(Freeman) But it was only a baby step toward life.

228
00:13:05,260 --> 00:13:10,100
在Miller和Urey的实验样品中
In all these samples saved from Miller and Urey's experiments,

229
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:13,100
Jeffrey Bada从来没能发现
Jeffrey Bada has never been able to detect

230
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:17,670
氨基酸组合在一起 组成蛋白质
amino acids joined together to make proteins.

231
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:19,840
你们制造的是简单的分子
What you're making is simple molecules,

232
00:13:19,840 --> 00:13:21,280
我们称之为单体
what we call monomers.

233
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:25,420
我们所知的生命 是由单体组成的
Life as we know it is made up of polymers,

234
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:29,350
高分子复杂单体构成
complex molecules that are made up of monomers.

235
00:13:29,350 --> 00:13:31,020
挑战仍然存在
And the challenge still remains

236
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:33,960
我们如能够把这些简单分子
how we can assemble these simple molecules

237
00:13:33,960 --> 00:13:38,100
装配成具有生物功能的 复杂分子
into complex molecules that have a biological function.

238
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:43,500
弗里曼：通向生命的 具有决定性的一布
(Freeman) That crucial step towards life

239
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:47,570
也许不在于实验室中 一个冒泡的烧瓶
may not come from a bubbling flask in a lab.

240
00:13:47,570 --> 00:13:49,370
Jen Blank确定
Jen Blank is sure of that

241
00:13:49,370 --> 00:13:52,310
因为她相信 生命在地球上的起源有其他因素
because she believes life needed something else

242
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:57,980
生命是以2万英里的速度
to get started on earth and it came from the sky...

243
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:01,450
从天而降...
At 20,000 miles per hour.

244
00:14:01,450 --> 00:14:03,590
Blank:我们知道，在太阳系的早期历史中
(Blank) We know that in the early history of the solar system,

245
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:05,960
彗星撞击地球
comets were slamming into the planets,

246
00:14:05,960 --> 00:14:08,430
彗星可能是辆货车
and maybe this would have been a vehicle

247
00:14:08,430 --> 00:14:11,730
给早期地球送来了 早期生命的原料
for delivering prebiotic materials to the early earth.

248
00:14:11,730 --> 00:14:14,570
弗里曼：彗星 --
(Freeman) Comets --

249
00:14:14,570 --> 00:14:18,740
如山一般大的冰块和灰尘堆 围绕着太阳运行
mountain-sized lumps of ice and dust circling the sun.

250
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:22,270
1999年
In 1999,

251
00:14:22,270 --> 00:14:25,980
NASA发射了名为"星尘"的飞行器
NASA sent a spacecraft called Stardust

252
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:29,480
它的任务是采集彗星碎片 并带回地球
to snag a piece of a comet and bring it back to earth.

253
00:14:30,650 --> 00:14:33,050
当科学家分析了这些材料
When scientists analyzed the material,

254
00:14:33,050 --> 00:14:36,750
发现它们含有氨基酸
they discovered that it contained amino acids,

255
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:39,090
构成蛋白质的原料
the building blocks of protein,

256
00:14:39,090 --> 00:14:42,330
生命的基本组织
the very tissue of life.

257
00:14:42,330 --> 00:14:44,260
突出的大问题之一是
One of the big outstanding questions

258
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:46,130
在彗星撞击地球的过程中
is whether or not organic compounds

259
00:14:46,130 --> 00:14:48,730
有机化合物能否
coming in on a comet and slamming into the earth

260
00:14:48,730 --> 00:14:50,630
挺得过撞击的条件
could survive the harsh conditions

261
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:52,140
残留下来
of that delivery experience.

262
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:54,710
所以我们在实验室里 设计了实验
And so we set out to test this in the laboratory.

263
00:14:54,710 --> 00:14:56,770
Freeman：Jen Blank在SETI工作
(Freeman) Jen Blank works at SETI,

264
00:14:56,770 --> 00:15:00,080
SETI，地外生命搜寻计划
the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence,

265
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:04,250
但她对外星人不感兴趣 她主要研究外来分子
but she's not interested in E.T. so much as alien molecules.

266
00:15:04,250 --> 00:15:07,850
她搞了个电脑模拟
So she's developed a computer simulation

267
00:15:07,850 --> 00:15:10,450
看看碰撞时发生什么
to see what happens to them on impact.

268
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:13,860
迎头而撞的彗星希望不大
Comets that hit earth head on are not very promising.

269
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:16,890
碰撞几乎完全将其烧成灰烬
The collision almost completely incinerates them.

270
00:15:16,890 --> 00:15:20,060
在这段影片里 蓝色是较低温度
In this movie,the blue colors are cold,

271
00:15:20,060 --> 00:15:23,000
向着红色区域，温度越来越热
and as you go toward the red,it gets hotter and hotter.

272
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:24,770
我们预料普通的碰撞
And so we expect the normal impact

273
00:15:24,770 --> 00:15:28,210
产生最极端的条件
to have the most extreme conditions.

274
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:31,710
你能看到，没有蓝色
You can see there's no blue color,

275
00:15:31,710 --> 00:15:36,350
材料肯定都要蒸发或挥发了
so essentially the material's all volatilized or vaporized.

276
00:15:36,350 --> 00:15:39,980
弗里曼：但是，如果彗星做一次 偏斜的碰撞
(Freeman) But when a comet makes a glancing blow,

277
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:42,150
它不会化为灰烬
it doesn't incinerate.

278
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:46,720
它会融化，将大量的水和氨基酸
It melts and dumps massive amounts of water and amino acids

279
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:48,660
散布到我们的原始星球
on our primeval planet.

280
00:15:48,660 --> 00:15:49,790
这次，我们试一下
This time,we're coming in

281
00:15:49,790 --> 00:15:51,360
和水平面15度的倾斜碰撞
at a 15-degree angle from the horizontal.

282
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:52,700
看到蓝色了
Watch the blue,

283
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:55,770
这里会有液态水
which will correspond to the liquid water.

284
00:15:55,770 --> 00:15:58,070
你可以看到大量的水跑掉了
So you can see a lot of the water's going away,

285
00:15:58,070 --> 00:16:00,700
但是仍然有大概20%的保留下来
but you still are retaining somewhere on the order of 20%

286
00:16:00,710 --> 00:16:03,110
以液态水的形式分散开
that's being delivered as liquid water.

287
00:16:03,110 --> 00:16:05,210
弗里曼：但是这些模拟
(Freeman) But these simulations

288
00:16:05,210 --> 00:16:07,280
只是追踪了彗星撞地球的
only track the temperature of the comet

289
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:09,380
温度分布
as it crashes to earth.

290
00:16:09,380 --> 00:16:11,980
要知道氨基酸会怎么样
To see what happens to the amino acids,

291
00:16:11,980 --> 00:16:15,950
Jen需要利用 强大的火力
Jen needs to get her hands on some serious firepower.

292
00:16:15,950 --> 00:16:17,390
如果你想给枪或 类似什么东西照个像
Here's where you might want to have

293
00:16:17,390 --> 00:16:19,220
就来这儿吧
a picture of a gun or something.

294
00:16:20,890 --> 00:16:25,730
弗里曼：这是Caltech的震动实验室
(Freeman) This is the shockwave lab at Caltech.

295
00:16:25,730 --> 00:16:27,660
实验室的自豪和骄傲
Its pride and joy --

296
00:16:27,670 --> 00:16:31,870
一个65英尺长的大炮 能够把发射的物体
a 65-foot-long gun capable of firing projectiles

297
00:16:31,870 --> 00:16:35,640
加速到超过每小时1万6千英里
at over 16,000 miles per hour.

298
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:39,040
这是Jen的子弹
And this is Jen's bullet.

299
00:16:41,610 --> 00:16:43,510
让我们把它当作彗星
Let's imagine this is a comet.

300
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:46,150
它是个金属罐 里面充满液体
It's a metal cannister with a liquid fill volume

301
00:16:46,150 --> 00:16:48,490
由水和溶解的氨基酸组成
that contains water and dissolved amino acids.

302
00:16:48,490 --> 00:16:49,650
在我们的实验里
And,in our experiment,

303
00:16:49,650 --> 00:16:51,720
我们用它来测试 彗星撞击地球时
we want to test the the response of this

304
00:16:51,720 --> 00:16:54,060
会发生什么情况
to a collision with an earth.

305
00:16:54,060 --> 00:16:56,230
这实际上是一个两段炮
This is actually a two-stage gun,

306
00:16:56,230 --> 00:16:57,530
在远端
and at the far end,

307
00:16:57,530 --> 00:17:00,500
我们用火药压缩气体
we use gunpowder to compress gas.

308
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:01,730
当火药点燃
The gunpowder's ignited,

309
00:17:01,730 --> 00:17:04,570
压缩空气猛烈冲击抛射体
and the compressed air slammed into the projectile,

310
00:17:04,570 --> 00:17:06,970
推着它穿过炮管
and sends it traveling down the barrel of the gun

311
00:17:06,970 --> 00:17:09,470
加速到大概每小时2千到3千英里
at velocities of around 2,000 to 3,000 miles per hour.

312
00:17:09,470 --> 00:17:12,210
这模拟了彗星和地球之间
This corresponds to an oblique-angle impact

313
00:17:12,210 --> 00:17:13,840
倾斜的碰撞
between a comet and a rocky Earth.

314
00:17:13,850 --> 00:17:16,910
弗里曼：为了模拟这场 天地大冲撞
(Freeman) To simulate this glancing interplanetary blow,

315
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:21,050
Jennifer控制了大炮的 最高出膛速度
Jennifer dials down the gun from its maximum muzzle speed

316
00:17:21,050 --> 00:17:24,860
祈祷她的罐子和里面的载货 能够幸存下来
and prays her cannister and its payload will survive.

317
00:17:24,860 --> 00:17:26,760
Blank:碰撞时产生的温度
(Blank) Temperatures in these impacts

318
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:30,660
能够高达上千摄氏度
would be essentially thousands of degrees centigrade.

319
00:17:30,660 --> 00:17:33,300
这可是真正极端的条件
So these are really extreme conditions.

320
00:17:47,510 --> 00:17:49,880
[ 哔哔声 ]
[ Beeping ]

321
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:59,090
某男:300伏，就位
(Man) 300 volts. Ready.

322
00:17:59,090 --> 00:18:00,690
[ 机械声 ]
[ Whirring ]

323
00:18:06,300 --> 00:18:07,460
闻到了么？
Smell that?

324
00:18:07,470 --> 00:18:09,830
那是枪药儿，枪药的味儿
That's the gunpowder,the gunpowder smell.

325
00:18:09,830 --> 00:18:12,140
实验容器被射入这个大罐子
The capsule is sent into this larger tank,

326
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:13,900
此罐被称为失而复得罐
which is called the recovery tank.

327
00:18:13,910 --> 00:18:15,440
大概有2吨重
It weighs about 2 tons,

328
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:17,340
它可真的能经住冲撞
and so it really muffles the collision.

329
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:19,680
而且，真的 在实验结尾
And,really,at the end of the experiment,

330
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:20,910
如果情况正常
if all things go well,

331
00:18:20,910 --> 00:18:23,550
它会和实验开始时差不多
it looks much the same as when we first started.

332
00:18:23,550 --> 00:18:27,080
弗里曼：现在，Jen 打开了实验容器
(Freeman) Now Jen breaks open the bullet capsule

333
00:18:27,080 --> 00:18:31,760
取样经过撞击的内容物
to retrieve its smashed contents and discover what happened

334
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:35,860
看看这些有机化合彗星汤 是什么情况
to this cometary soup of organic chemicals.

335
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:38,960
这是一个样本 其中有两种不同的氨基酸
And here's an example with two different amino acids in it --

336
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:40,960
氨基乙酸，最简单的氨基酸
glycine,which is the simplest amino acid,

337
00:18:40,970 --> 00:18:42,600
还有脯氨酸，另一种氨基酸
and proline,which is another one.

338
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:45,170
这是最初的溶液 你看，只有两种成分
And here's the initial solution. You can see just two.

339
00:18:45,170 --> 00:18:47,700
这是后来的溶液分析
Here's an analysis of the solution afterwards.

340
00:18:47,710 --> 00:18:49,170
最主要的副产品
The most high-abundance byproducts

341
00:18:49,170 --> 00:18:50,610
看来是两种简单氨基酸的
turned out to be all combinations

342
00:18:50,610 --> 00:18:53,540
组合产物
of the first two amino acids.

343
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:55,480
我们真的很兴奋
So we were actually very excited

344
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:57,250
因为碰撞导致的反应
because the reactions that were occurring

345
00:18:57,250 --> 00:18:59,750
确实产生了更大的 生物学意义的分子
were actually forming larger biologically relevant molecules.

346
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:04,320
弗里曼：Jen的大威力实验表明
(Freeman) Jen's high-powered experiment

347
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:06,620
彗星也许真的撞击早期地球
suggests comets may have bludgeoned early Earth

348
00:19:06,620 --> 00:19:09,890
使之向生命更近一步
one step closer to life.

349
00:19:09,890 --> 00:19:13,230
它们帮助氨基酸结合
They helped amino acids join together,

350
00:19:13,230 --> 00:19:16,630
甚至可能组成原始蛋白质
perhaps even forming primitive proteins.

351
00:19:16,630 --> 00:19:19,270
在构建更大的 生物学意义上的分子试验中
We're actually harnessing the power of the impact

352
00:19:19,270 --> 00:19:22,340
我们控制了撞击的能量
to build larger biologically relevant molecules.

353
00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:24,640
这可能是构成原始生命的
So this could have been a dominant source

354
00:19:24,640 --> 00:19:27,240
基本材料的主要来源
of the building blocks that led to the origin of life.

355
00:19:31,650 --> 00:19:34,580
弗里曼：我们通常以为 40亿年前，我们的星球是个
(Freeman) We used to think our planet was a hellhole

356
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:36,420
危险的地方
4 billion years ago,

357
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:40,620
任何生命的火花都会被 马上扑灭
where any spark of life would have been instantly incinerated.

358
00:19:40,630 --> 00:19:44,090
现在，我们了解到 彗星里有生命的化学成分
Now we know the chemicals of life were inside comets,

359
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:46,460
如雨般落向我们的星球
raining down on our planet.

360
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:48,430
它们并未坠毁燃烧
And they didn't crash and burn.

361
00:19:48,430 --> 00:19:50,300
它们兴旺昌盛
They thrived.

362
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:53,100
但是其它的生命原料还没着落
But other ingredients of life are still missing.

363
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:55,840
位居通缉名单首位的是DNA
At the top of the wanted list is DNA,

364
00:19:55,840 --> 00:19:59,240
携带我们基因信息的分子
the molecule that carries our genetic identity.

365
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:02,780
现在，一个人可以 破解遗传密码
Now at last,one man may have cracked the code

366
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:05,620
明确最初的DNA排列
to the origins of DNA itself.

367
00:20:12,270 --> 00:20:14,770
弗里曼：生命进化成为
(Freeman) It's taken 4 billion years

368
00:20:14,770 --> 00:20:16,070
如同你我的复杂有机体
for life to evolve

369
00:20:16,070 --> 00:20:18,870
花了40亿年的时间
into organisms as complex as you and me.

370
00:20:18,870 --> 00:20:20,440
我们处于
We're at the tip

371
00:20:20,440 --> 00:20:23,740
进化树的顶端
of a tall branch of the tree of life.

372
00:20:23,750 --> 00:20:27,450
在大树的根部深处 是微生物
Down in the deepest roots are microbes

373
00:20:27,450 --> 00:20:30,480
只由单细胞组成
whose bodies are just a single cell.

374
00:20:30,490 --> 00:20:34,560
但是微生物的生理活动 就像我们一样
But each microbe's biology is just like ours.

375
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:37,120
无论看上去有多么不同
No matter how different it looks,

376
00:20:37,130 --> 00:20:42,160
共性在于那一缕DNA
its identity resides in a strand of DNA.

377
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:47,600
进化树中，每一种 我们所知的生命体都是如此
Every living organism we know belongs somewhere on this tree.

378
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:53,270
但是为什么进化树 植根于这最初的土壤
But why did the tree of life grow in the first place?

379
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:58,310
要解决这个神秘问题 我们要先找到种子
To solve that mystery,we have to find the seed.

380
00:20:58,310 --> 00:21:02,480
科学家对进化树的种子 所知不多
Scientists don't know much about this seed,

381
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:05,550
但他们对一件事很肯定
but they are sure about one thing.

382
00:21:05,550 --> 00:21:11,190
地球上的每个生物体 都有一个共同点
Every living organism on earth shares one common feature --

383
00:21:11,190 --> 00:21:12,490
都有一张膜
a tough outer layer

384
00:21:12,490 --> 00:21:15,230
将它们和外部的世界分隔开
that separates it from the world outside.

385
00:21:15,230 --> 00:21:18,470
每个细胞都有细胞膜
Every cell has a membrane.

386
00:21:18,470 --> 00:21:21,840
生命的第一颗树种也 一定有细胞膜
The first seeds of life must have had one,too.

387
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:25,140
呃，我们认为，我们需要 这种原始的细胞膜
Well,we think we need this kind of primitive cell membrane

388
00:21:25,140 --> 00:21:28,540
这样才能把基因分子裹在里面
to keep the genetic molecules trapped inside.

389
00:21:28,540 --> 00:21:31,010
不可能让这些东西 流的到处都是啊
You can't just have everything diffusing around.

390
00:21:31,010 --> 00:21:33,250
一定要有这种分隔
You have to have this compartmentalized.

391
00:21:34,780 --> 00:21:37,220
弗里曼：哈佛医学院的Jack Szostak
(Freeman) Jack Szostak at Harvard Medical School

392
00:21:37,220 --> 00:21:41,220
正致力于解决生命的最大谜团
is on a quest to solve one of life's biggest mysteries --

393
00:21:41,220 --> 00:21:45,860
早期的生命形式是如何 为自己筑起高墙壁垒的
how the earliest life-forms walled themselves in,

394
00:21:45,860 --> 00:21:50,100
从"非我"中定义出"我"
defined "me" from "not me."

395
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:53,700
Szostak:嗯，现代的细胞膜 非常坚韧
(Szostak) So,modern cell membranes are really tough,

396
00:21:53,700 --> 00:21:56,800
它们很稳定，是很好的壁垒
they're stable,they're great barriers,

397
00:21:56,810 --> 00:22:00,910
它们控制着各种物质的进出
they allow cells to control everything that gets in and out.

398
00:22:00,910 --> 00:22:04,910
但这需要很多进化 高度演化的构造
But that requires a lot of fancy,highly evolved machinery,

399
00:22:04,910 --> 00:22:08,550
和最初细胞的定义不一样
which wasn't around by definition for the first cells.

400
00:22:08,550 --> 00:22:11,520
细胞膜已经大不相同了
So those membranes had to be really different.

401
00:22:11,520 --> 00:22:15,460
弗里曼：但是，这些包裹原始细胞的细胞膜
(Freeman) But what might these primitive skins around the cells

402
00:22:15,460 --> 00:22:16,860
会是什么样呢？
have looked like?

403
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:18,760
Jack从肥皂泡中
Jack found inspiration...

404
00:22:22,060 --> 00:22:24,200
找到了灵感
...In soap bubbles.

405
00:22:25,070 --> 00:22:27,740
Szostak:嗯，这很脆弱
(Szostak) So,these are delicate.

406
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:29,600
它们阐释了这个想法
They really illustrate the idea

407
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:31,870
那就是有一个隔离的空间
that there's an encapsulated space.

408
00:22:31,870 --> 00:22:33,710
[ 咯咯 ]
[ Chuckles ]

409
00:22:33,710 --> 00:22:34,780
这很棒
That was pretty good.

410
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:38,750
弗里曼：肥皂泡是由脂肪酸分子
(Freeman) Soap bubbles are made

411
00:22:38,750 --> 00:22:42,020
组成的
from molecules called fatty acids,

412
00:22:42,020 --> 00:22:43,350
Jack认为
primitive chemicals

413
00:22:43,350 --> 00:22:46,520
在早期地球的热水泉中
that Jack thinks were produced on the primeval Earth

414
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:49,620
会产生这些原始化学物质
inside hydrothermal geysers.

415
00:22:49,620 --> 00:22:51,730
某些矿物质会有催化作用
The right kinds of minerals

416
00:22:51,730 --> 00:22:55,060
将简单的物质，比如一氧化碳 甲烷，水
could catalyze the assembly of fatty acids

417
00:22:55,060 --> 00:23:00,330
转化成为脂肪酸
from simple things like carbon monoxide,methane,water.

418
00:23:00,340 --> 00:23:02,600
这是在热液泉口
One kind of nice way that that could happen

419
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:04,840
很有可能发生的事情
is in a hydrothermal-vent system,

420
00:23:04,840 --> 00:23:07,140
而泡泡会升上表面
and then they could bubble up to the surface.

421
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:18,590
弗里曼：Jack在实验室里
(Freeman) So Jack set about re-creating

422
00:23:18,590 --> 00:23:21,620
模拟热水泉
the chemistry of a geyser in his lab

423
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:25,390
并最终创造了脂肪酸
and eventually created fatty acids.

424
00:23:25,390 --> 00:23:29,060
现在，他把脂肪酸混入 原始化学浓汤
Now he mixes them into a primordial chemical soup

425
00:23:29,060 --> 00:23:32,430
汤的配方是水，盐和氨基酸
made of water,salt,and amino acids,

426
00:23:32,430 --> 00:23:34,600
他目击了一次
and he watches

427
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:38,440
异乎寻常的转变
as a remarkable transformation takes place.

428
00:23:38,440 --> 00:23:41,640
产生了一片片的膜状物 像波浪，有起伏
So,membranes form in sheets,and they're kind of wavy,

429
00:23:41,640 --> 00:23:43,910
边缘是某种高能量
and the edges are kind of high energy.

430
00:23:43,910 --> 00:23:46,650
接下来它们自我封闭
So what happens is they close up on themselves,

431
00:23:46,650 --> 00:23:49,280
制造出了小规模球形结构
and they make little round structures.

432
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:52,450
它们成了封闭结构 像是小肥皂泡
So they're closed structures,like tiny soap bubbles.

433
00:23:54,660 --> 00:23:59,760
弗里曼：这些小点点 实际上是空泡泡
(Freeman) These little dots are actually hollow bubbles,

434
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:03,360
直径小于千分之一英寸
less than a thousandth of an inch across.

435
00:24:03,370 --> 00:24:06,870
这就是40亿年前，最初的活细胞
This is what the first living cells may have looked like

436
00:24:06,870 --> 00:24:09,340
可能的样子
4 billion years ago.

437
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:14,280
Jack也许在他的显微镜下
Jack may have re-created the seeds of life

438
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:17,010
制造了生命之种
right under his microscope.

439
00:24:17,010 --> 00:24:20,710
但是，如果细胞不能完成 一项严酷的任务
But it is impossible for life to evolve

440
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:24,450
生命就不可能进化
unless cells can pull off one crucial task.

441
00:24:24,450 --> 00:24:29,420
它们必须要能够生长和分裂
They must be able to grow and divide.

442
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:32,430
就像种子生长开花
This is how seeds blossom into flowers,

443
00:24:32,430 --> 00:24:35,260
毛毛虫化茧成蝶
how a caterpillar becomes a butterfly,

444
00:24:35,260 --> 00:24:38,930
小娃娃生长成人
and how a baby becomes an adult.

445
00:24:38,930 --> 00:24:41,240
肥皂泡生长并分裂
Soap bubbles grow and divide

446
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,640
内部却近乎只有一团空气
with nothing more than a puff of air,

447
00:24:43,640 --> 00:24:47,540
Jack轻轻的摇晃这些小泡
so Jack slightly jiggles the vesicles

448
00:24:47,540 --> 00:24:50,710
看到不可思议的事发生了
and watches something incredible happen.

449
00:24:50,710 --> 00:24:54,210
Szostak:这些膜会开始 自发的生长
(Szostak) The membrane will start to grow spontaneously.

450
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:56,050
我们在显微镜下可以看到
What we see in the microscope

451
00:24:56,050 --> 00:24:58,990
它以特有的方式增长
is that it grows in a very peculiar way.

452
00:24:58,990 --> 00:25:03,360
初始的小泡变成一个 长条形，有弹性的管子
The whole initial vesicle turned into a long,flexible tube.

453
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:06,590
弗里曼：这个脂肪酸膜
(Freeman) These fatty-acid membranes

454
00:25:06,590 --> 00:25:10,230
完成了进化史上 最重要的任务之一
are achieving one of evolution's most essential jobs --

455
00:25:10,230 --> 00:25:12,130
自我复制
self-replication.

456
00:25:12,130 --> 00:25:15,000
看上去它们自动完成这些
And they appear to do it automatically.

457
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,170
对Jack来说，
To Jack,this is a sure sign

458
00:25:17,170 --> 00:25:19,240
这是他接近理解生命奇迹 起始之谜的
he's getting close to understanding

459
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:21,680
一个信号
how the miracle of life began.

460
00:25:21,680 --> 00:25:23,840
我们有了一个 生长和分裂的周期
So we have a cycle of growth and division

461
00:25:23,850 --> 00:25:29,520
这和原始细胞的 生长和分裂很相似
that's very much like primitive cell growth and division.

462
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:32,950
弗里曼：Jack可能已经找到了
(Freeman) Jack may very well have found the recipe

463
00:25:32,950 --> 00:25:35,690
生命最早期细胞结构的配方
for life's earliest cell structure,

464
00:25:35,690 --> 00:25:37,990
但是，要得到真正的生命
but to be truly alive,

465
00:25:37,990 --> 00:25:41,330
这些细胞需要极其重要的组分
those cells need one vital ingredient --

466
00:25:41,330 --> 00:25:43,460
基因
genes.

467
00:25:43,470 --> 00:25:46,230
基因是分子特征
Genes are the molecular identity

468
00:25:46,230 --> 00:25:49,840
从一个细胞传递到它的复制品
that can pass from a cell to its copy.

469
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:54,980
所有现代生命通过 DNA双螺旋结构完成这一任务
All modern life does this with a double helix of DNA.

470
00:25:54,980 --> 00:25:58,380
这是我们所知的 最复杂的化学分子结构
It's the most complex chemical molecule we know,

471
00:25:58,380 --> 00:26:01,520
由几千万原子组成
made up of tens of billions of atoms.

472
00:26:01,520 --> 00:26:04,750
DNA控制着每一个生命的 每一个细节
DNA controls every detail of every living thing --

473
00:26:04,750 --> 00:26:06,350
我们眼睛的颜色
the color of our eyes,

474
00:26:06,350 --> 00:26:08,360
树上叶子的形状
the shape of the leaves on a tree,

475
00:26:08,360 --> 00:26:12,160
甚至是最简单细菌的运动方式
the way even the most simple bacterium swims.

476
00:26:12,160 --> 00:26:14,500
细胞每次分裂时
And every time a cell divides,

477
00:26:14,500 --> 00:26:17,030
它会在新产生的2个细胞中
it places a copy of its DNA

478
00:26:17,030 --> 00:26:21,640
放置同样的DNA复制品
in both cells of the new generation.

479
00:26:21,640 --> 00:26:25,540
在英格兰，曼彻斯特大学
At the University of Manchester in England,

480
00:26:25,540 --> 00:26:27,670
化学家John Sutherland
chemist John Sutherland

481
00:26:27,680 --> 00:26:30,640
尝试发现DNA怎样产生
is trying to discover how DNA came to be --

482
00:26:30,650 --> 00:26:34,880
这是明确我们如何而来的关键
the key to discovering how we came to be.

483
00:26:34,880 --> 00:26:37,250
Sutherland:对我来说，真正有趣的地方是
(Sutherland) For me,the really interesting point here

484
00:26:37,250 --> 00:26:39,820
化学和生命之间的过渡
is the transition between chemistry and biology.

485
00:26:39,820 --> 00:26:41,090
以前，人们总是假设
It's always been assumed

486
00:26:41,090 --> 00:26:43,590
需要有一种信息化的分子
that you need to have an informational molecule.

487
00:26:43,590 --> 00:26:46,560
如果没有遗传功能 就没有真正的生命
You can't really have life unless you can have inheritance,

488
00:26:46,560 --> 00:26:48,800
所以你需要一些东西
so you need to have something which you can inherit

489
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:50,530
能够通过分子里储存的信息 完成遗传
the information stored in a molecule.

490
00:26:50,530 --> 00:26:53,770
弗里曼：像DNA一样复杂的分子
(Freeman) A molecule as complex as DNA

491
00:26:53,770 --> 00:26:55,770
也许永远不可能在 原始生命池塘中
could never have formed all by itself

492
00:26:55,770 --> 00:26:57,810
自我构建
in a primordial pond.

493
00:26:57,810 --> 00:27:00,240
但是，有一个简单版本
But there is a simpler version of it --

494
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:02,980
单螺旋的信息分子
a single-stranded,informational molecule

495
00:27:02,980 --> 00:27:07,950
称作核糖核酸，RNA
called ribonucleic acid,or RNA.

496
00:27:07,950 --> 00:27:10,950
研究生命起源的科学家 长久以来相信
Scientists studying the origin of life

497
00:27:10,950 --> 00:27:15,560
RNA是DNA的前辈
have long believed that RNA might be the precursor of DNA,

498
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:18,960
最初的生命基因密码携带者
a simpler carrier of life's genetic code.

499
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:21,660
所以，游戏的名字就是 如果你愿意
And so the name of the game,if you like,

500
00:27:21,660 --> 00:27:25,300
在早期地球的环境条件下
is to make RNA from very,very simple precursor chemicals,

501
00:27:25,300 --> 00:27:26,800
利用简单的有机化学
using simple organic chemistry

502
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:29,040
从非常非常简单的 原始化学物质中
under conditions which could have prevailed

503
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:30,100
制作RNA
on the early earth.

504
00:27:30,110 --> 00:27:33,710
弗里曼：RNA是一个巨大的分子链
(Freeman) RNA is a giant molecular string

505
00:27:33,710 --> 00:27:37,540
由4种基本组分组成
made up of four different basic building blocks.

506
00:27:37,550 --> 00:27:40,680
这些基本组分的排列顺序
The order in which these blocks are arranged

507
00:27:40,680 --> 00:27:43,180
就构成了基因密码
forms a genetic code.

508
00:27:43,180 --> 00:27:46,320
作为一个化学家，当你看着RNA
When you look at RNA,as a chemist,

509
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:49,960
你会很惊讶，的确
you're in sort of astonishment,really,

510
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:51,930
这是多么奇妙的分子结构啊
at just what a wonderful molecule it is.

511
00:27:51,930 --> 00:27:52,960
相当复杂
It's complex.

512
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:54,560
相当美妙的结构
It's a really beautiful structure.

513
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:55,960
你一定想知道——
And you inevitably wonder --

514
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:58,000
这种结构是如何成型的?
how on earth did that structure arise?

515
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,400
化学是究竟如何创造出它的?
How on earth did chemistry produce it?

516
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:03,840
弗里曼：RNA的结构看上去简单
(Freeman) RNA's structure looks simple,

517
00:28:03,840 --> 00:28:05,940
但是眼睛会有错觉
but looks can deceive.

518
00:28:05,940 --> 00:28:09,640
每一个基本组分实际上由 两部分组成
Each building block is actually made of two parts --

519
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:13,580
一个糖分子和一个核基
a sugar molecule and a nuclear base.

520
00:28:13,580 --> 00:28:16,780
Sutherland:化学家发现 他们可以制作这些核基
(Sutherland) Chemists found they could make the nuclear bases,

521
00:28:16,780 --> 00:28:18,190
当他们意识到
and so,when they then realized

522
00:28:18,190 --> 00:28:19,950
他们可以制作这些糖
they could actually make the sugars,

523
00:28:19,950 --> 00:28:22,490
他们就想，“我们一定可以 把它们组合在一起”
they just thought,"we must be able to join them together."

524
00:28:22,490 --> 00:28:24,190
于是他们尝试了很多年
And so they tried for many years,

525
00:28:24,190 --> 00:28:25,660
但是，问题是，从化学上来讲
but the problem was,chemically,

526
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:27,330
你就是不可能把它们组合起来
you just can't join them together.

527
00:28:27,330 --> 00:28:31,230
弗里曼：很多年来，科学家不断尝试
(Freeman) For years,scientists tried and failed

528
00:28:31,230 --> 00:28:32,930
希望通过把糖和核基
to whip up some RNA

529
00:28:32,930 --> 00:28:36,800
放在一个加热容器里 组成某种RNA
by placing a sugar and a base in a pot

530
00:28:36,800 --> 00:28:39,240
但都失败了
and heating it up.

531
00:28:39,240 --> 00:28:42,810
但是John意识到 原始浓汤的做法
But John realized that the primordial soup metaphor

532
00:28:42,810 --> 00:28:44,310
太简单了
was too narrow.

533
00:28:44,310 --> 00:28:48,120
早期地球的厨房里 可不是只有火炉而已
Early Earth's kitchen had more than just a stovetop.

534
00:28:48,120 --> 00:28:52,290
那里还有烤箱，汽锅和冰箱
It had an oven,a steamer,and a freezer.

535
00:28:52,290 --> 00:28:55,620
就像有一个池塘
This is like a pond that's sitting there for a while,

536
00:28:55,620 --> 00:28:56,920
然后温度上升
and then the temperature goes up,

537
00:28:56,930 --> 00:28:58,330
池水开始蒸发
and the pond starts to evaporate.

538
00:28:58,330 --> 00:29:00,890
池塘蒸发的残余物
And the residue that's left after the pond's evaporated

539
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:02,330
又被继续加热一段时间
is then heated for a period.

540
00:29:02,330 --> 00:29:03,600
天又开始下雨
And then it rains again.

541
00:29:03,600 --> 00:29:05,670
雨住日出
And after it rains,the sun comes out.

542
00:29:05,670 --> 00:29:07,630
循环往复
So it's a sequence of events,

543
00:29:07,640 --> 00:29:10,470
条件在变化 而不是静态的
rather than one static set of conditions.

544
00:29:10,470 --> 00:29:13,140
弗里曼：John和他的团队
(Freeman) So John and his team re-create

545
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:16,640
重现了条件的变化 加湿、干燥、加热、冷却
the sequence of wetting,drying,heating,and cooling

546
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:19,410
那些在早期地球 可能发生的情况
that would have taken place on early Earth.

547
00:29:19,410 --> 00:29:22,550
有史以来第一次
And incredibly,for the first time,

548
00:29:22,550 --> 00:29:27,550
这些化学家取得了 前所未有的成就
these chemists achieve what none before them ever did --

549
00:29:27,560 --> 00:29:33,030
他们创造出了RNA 四种基本组分中的两种
they create two of the four basic building blocks of RNA.

550
00:29:33,030 --> 00:29:35,560
好，我们现在找到了 制造两种组分的方法
Well,now we have to find a way to make the other two,

551
00:29:35,560 --> 00:29:37,330
我认为我们离成功很近了
and I think we're pretty close to doing that.

552
00:29:37,330 --> 00:29:39,500
然后，我们打算把它们串起来
And then we want to string them together

553
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:41,740
制造一个RNA分子链
to make an RNA polymer molecule.

554
00:29:41,740 --> 00:29:43,640
我们最近确实找到了一些方法
And we've actually recently found some ways

555
00:29:43,640 --> 00:29:45,640
我们认为可以串起它们
in which we think we can string them together.

556
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:51,240
但是我们知道 我们极其复杂的生理结构
But we know our incredibly complex biology

557
00:29:51,250 --> 00:29:54,680
不可能是 地球上最初的生命形式
could not have been the first form of life on earth.

558
00:29:54,680 --> 00:29:58,150
我们已知的生命形式之前 生命是什么样的？
What was life like before the life we know?

559
00:29:58,150 --> 00:30:02,260
找到这种生命，可以帮助 我们理解生命何来
Finding that life might help us understand how we got here,

560
00:30:02,260 --> 00:30:05,530
而问题的答案 也许就在鼻子底下
and the answer could be right under our noses.

561
00:30:05,530 --> 00:30:09,060
所有现存的生命 都有同样的祖先
All life so far started as the same life.

562
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:10,800
但是没有迹象表明
But it's never been clear to me

563
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:12,900
在这个星球上，不能同时存在一种以上的
that you can't have more than one form of life

564
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:14,300
生命形式
on the planet at the same time.

565
00:30:14,300 --> 00:30:15,570
我认为，这就像是
I think it's entirely likely

566
00:30:15,570 --> 00:30:17,640
我们和一个真正的 异形生命
that we share this planet

567
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:19,970
共同生活在地球上
with a genuinely alien type of life --

568
00:30:19,970 --> 00:30:22,680
"异形"并不是指它来自天外
alien not because it necessarily came from space,

569
00:30:22,680 --> 00:30:25,780
而是因为它属于 另外一棵生命树
but because it belongs to a different tree of life

570
00:30:25,780 --> 00:30:27,110
与你我不同
from you or me.

571
00:30:27,110 --> 00:30:28,280
足够引人注目
Remarkably enough,

572
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:30,450
没有人想到寻找这种生命
it turns out that nobody has really thought

573
00:30:30,450 --> 00:30:33,020
因为我们对它一无所知
to look for life on earth as we don't know it.

574
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:35,920
弗里曼：Paul Davies是世界上
(Freeman) Paul Davies is

575
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:38,690
顶尖的宇宙学家之一
one of the world's leading cosmologists.

576
00:30:38,690 --> 00:30:41,560
他承认自己不是生物学家
He's the first to admit he's not a biologist,

577
00:30:41,560 --> 00:30:44,330
但他无惧进入他们的领域
but he's not afraid to venture into their territory

578
00:30:44,330 --> 00:30:47,700
提出没人想到的问题
and ask questions no one else thought to ask.

579
00:30:47,700 --> 00:30:50,870
我们不知道 无生命的化学混合物
We don't know how a mix of nonliving chemicals

580
00:30:50,870 --> 00:30:52,570
如何就转变成生命
turns itself into a living thing.

581
00:30:52,570 --> 00:30:53,840
我们甚至不知道
We don't even know

582
00:30:53,840 --> 00:30:55,480
事情真相是否按此顺序
whether this is a very likely sequence of events

583
00:30:55,480 --> 00:30:57,710
或者不是按此顺序
or very unlikely sequence of events.

584
00:30:57,710 --> 00:31:00,450
但是让我们假设是这样的
But let's suppose it's very likely.

585
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:03,250
那么在地球上，这样的事情
Then shouldn't it have happened many times over

586
00:31:03,250 --> 00:31:04,950
是不是发生了很多遍?
right here on earth?

587
00:31:04,950 --> 00:31:08,920
弗里曼：Paul把可能的 本土异形生物
(Freeman) Paul's term for possible homegrown alien life

588
00:31:08,920 --> 00:31:11,860
称为"影子生物圈"
is "the shadow biosphere."

589
00:31:11,860 --> 00:31:15,200
他有一个发现计划
And he has a plan for how we might discover it.

590
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:18,100
Davies:我们可以考察地球上那些
(Davies) We could look at places on earth

591
00:31:18,100 --> 00:31:20,630
条件如此极端、如此恶劣的地方
where conditions are so extreme,so harsh,

592
00:31:20,630 --> 00:31:23,600
普通已知生物不会生活在那里
they're beyond the reach of life as we know it

593
00:31:23,600 --> 00:31:26,370
我们可以看看是否有 异形微生物
to see if there's some hardy alien type of microorganism

594
00:31:26,370 --> 00:31:27,470
生活在那里
living there.

595
00:31:31,810 --> 00:31:35,520
弗里曼：Paul的同事之一 Felisa Wolfe-Simon,
(Freeman) One of Paul's colleagues,Felisa Wolfe-Simon,

596
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:38,390
正在通过挖泥巴
is looking for a shadow biosphere

597
00:31:38,390 --> 00:31:41,490
寻找影子生物圈
by digging through the mud.

598
00:31:41,490 --> 00:31:46,590
我们在这些泥巴里 可能找到的生物
So,the life that we might find in,let's say,this much mud --

599
00:31:46,590 --> 00:31:48,660
也许有几十亿不同的微生物
we could have billions of different microbes

600
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:52,430
就像你我和蚊子不同，它们之间也毫不相同
that are as different as you and I are to a mosquito.

601
00:31:52,430 --> 00:31:54,640
实际上，我们和蚊子的关系
In fact,we are more closely related to mosquitoes

602
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:55,770
要比它们彼此关系更接近
than they are to each other.

603
00:31:55,770 --> 00:31:57,400
这些微生物就是如此不同
That's how different these microbes are.

604
00:31:57,410 --> 00:32:01,140
弗里曼：Felisa在加州Menlo国家公园
(Freeman) Felisa works at the U.S. Geological Survey

605
00:32:01,140 --> 00:32:03,280
为美国地调局工作
in Menlo Park,California,

606
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:06,380
但是资助她研究泥巴的
but it's NASA's astrobiology program

607
00:32:06,380 --> 00:32:08,820
是NASA的航空项目
that pays her to study mud.

608
00:32:08,820 --> 00:32:11,050
Wolfe-Simon:我每到一个新地方
(Wolfe-Simon) So,one of the things I always do

609
00:32:11,050 --> 00:32:13,050
我总会做一件事
when I go to a new environment --

610
00:32:13,050 --> 00:32:15,460
无论是在世界上的什么地方
regardless of where it is in the world --

611
00:32:15,460 --> 00:32:18,530
我都会取样品 建立一个Winogradsky系统
I take samples to set up a Winogradsky Column.

612
00:32:18,530 --> 00:32:20,490
弗里曼：Winogradsky系统
(Freeman) A Winogradsky Column

613
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:22,930
就像是浓缩的地球历史
is like a potted history of the earth,

614
00:32:22,930 --> 00:32:27,570
是各种奇怪微生物的乐土
a breeding ground for all kinds of strange microbes.

615
00:32:27,570 --> 00:32:29,200
Wolfe-Simon:你取了泥土样品
(Wolfe-Simon) You take your sample of mud

616
00:32:29,200 --> 00:32:33,240
你把泥巴放进一个玻璃罐
and you just fill,say,a glass jar,

617
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:35,080
把它放在窗户上
and you put it in the window.

618
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:36,480
你会观察它
You'll see over time,

619
00:32:36,480 --> 00:32:40,210
在这个Winogradsky系统里 漂亮的颜色在演化
beautiful colors evolve in this Winogradsky Column.

620
00:32:40,210 --> 00:32:44,380
弗里曼：颜色的变化显示了
(Freeman) The progression of colors reveals

621
00:32:44,390 --> 00:32:48,520
这些罐生微生物的准确类型
distinct types of microbes that are inhabiting the column.

622
00:32:48,520 --> 00:32:52,690
一种微生物 需要特定的生存因素
Every type of microbe needs specific elements to survive.

623
00:32:52,690 --> 00:32:57,060
有些依靠阳光尾声 另一些却嗜碳
Some feed on sunlight,others on carbon.

624
00:32:57,070 --> 00:33:00,470
更奇怪的虫子吃硫磺
More exotic bugs feed on sulfur.

625
00:33:02,440 --> 00:33:05,710
但是Felisa在2009年 取的一个泥巴样本
But one mud sample Felisa took in 2009

626
00:33:05,710 --> 00:33:10,140
里面的虫子要比 她能想象的更奇怪
revealed bugs stranger than she could have ever imagined.

627
00:33:10,140 --> 00:33:11,910
它来自于一个 对地球上几乎所有生物
It came from a place

628
00:33:11,910 --> 00:33:15,720
都是高度有毒的地方
that is highly toxic to almost all life on earth --

629
00:33:15,720 --> 00:33:19,420
Mono湖，加利福尼亚
Mono Lake in California.

630
00:33:19,420 --> 00:33:22,760
谈起Mono湖 你会情不自禁的有些思念
You can't talk about Mono Lake without being a little wistful.

631
00:33:22,760 --> 00:33:24,790
你会感觉身处另一个星球
You feel like you're on another planet.

632
00:33:24,790 --> 00:33:27,060
这里还有非常有趣的混合物
It also contains very interesting compounds.

633
00:33:27,060 --> 00:33:30,760
我最感兴趣的是那里的 砷含量非常高
Of particular interest to me was very high levels of arsenic.

634
00:33:30,770 --> 00:33:33,970
那大概是美国国家环保局 建议的砷含量的
There's roughly 40,000 times or so

635
00:33:33,970 --> 00:33:36,800
4万多倍
the recommended arsenic from,say,the E.P.A.

636
00:33:36,800 --> 00:33:39,110
自然，在我看来
So it seemed to me logically

637
00:33:39,110 --> 00:33:42,880
这是一个影子生物圈
that it could harbor potentially the vestiges

638
00:33:42,880 --> 00:33:44,610
可能的遗迹所在地
of a shadow biosphere.

639
00:33:44,610 --> 00:33:46,880
弗里曼：当Felisa挖起Mono湖的泥巴后
(Freeman) Felisa was not disappointed

640
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:49,280
她没有失望
when she dug in the mud of Mono Lake.

641
00:33:49,280 --> 00:33:52,450
她真的找到了能够在
She did,indeed,find bugs

642
00:33:52,450 --> 00:33:56,290
富砷的有毒环境中生存的微生物
that could survive these highly toxic doses of arsenic.

643
00:33:56,290 --> 00:33:59,130
她新发现的这些喜欢 有毒环境的微生物
And her newfound interest in this poison

644
00:33:59,130 --> 00:34:02,700
不仅引起了她同行的注意
didn't just cause ripples with her scientific colleagues.

645
00:34:02,700 --> 00:34:03,730
有一天，我回到家
I came home one day,

646
00:34:03,730 --> 00:34:05,200
我买了这些书
and I brought all these books in,

647
00:34:05,200 --> 00:34:06,700
把他们放在电脑上
and I put them on my counter.

648
00:34:06,700 --> 00:34:09,170
我的丈夫说，"那么，你为什么对砷化物这么感兴趣"
My husband said,"so,what are you interested in arsenic for?"

649
00:34:09,170 --> 00:34:12,170
我说，"呃，我对它的解除机理感兴趣
And I said,"well,I'm interested in how it dissolves,

650
00:34:12,170 --> 00:34:15,240
你会发现的."
where you might find it."

651
00:34:15,240 --> 00:34:18,380
我丈夫有点不安，说
And my husband,a little disturbed,said,

652
00:34:18,380 --> 00:34:20,210
"但是你没有研究砷化物"
"but you don't study arsenic."

653
00:34:20,210 --> 00:34:23,550
于是我开始咯咯笑
And then I started to giggle

654
00:34:23,550 --> 00:34:27,120
我意识到他有点紧张
'cause I realized he was getting a bit nervous.

655
00:34:31,230 --> 00:34:34,830
弗里曼：对大多数生物体来说 砷化物都是很高效的毒药
(Freeman) Arsenic is an effective poison to most organisms

656
00:34:34,830 --> 00:34:38,630
因为它非常接近磷元素
because it closely resembles the element phosphorus.

657
00:34:38,630 --> 00:34:40,200
它欺骗我们的细胞
It tricks our cells

658
00:34:40,200 --> 00:34:43,140
用一种元素代替另一种
into substituting one element for the other.

659
00:34:43,140 --> 00:34:46,470
由于磷元素构成了DNA的主干
Since phosphorus forms the backbone of DNA,

660
00:34:46,470 --> 00:34:48,340
所以砷的影响是毁灭性的
its effects are devastating.

661
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:51,610
但对Felisa找到的虫子无效
But not the bugs that Felisa found.

662
00:34:51,610 --> 00:34:55,020
无论她给虫子 多大剂量的砷化物
No matter how big a dose of arsenic she gave them,

663
00:34:55,020 --> 00:34:56,320
甚至达到Mono湖最高标准的
even many times more

664
00:34:56,320 --> 00:34:58,790
几倍之多
than the sky-high levels in Mono Lake,

665
00:34:58,790 --> 00:35:01,560
这些微生物还过得很滋润
the microbes just kept on growing.

666
00:35:01,560 --> 00:35:03,990
Wolfe-Simon:所以，有些微生物
(Wolfe-Simon) So these are microbes using

667
00:35:03,990 --> 00:35:07,290
利用看上去是有毒性的物质
what seems to be poison or toxic substances,

668
00:35:07,300 --> 00:35:09,430
以此繁殖生存
and this biology is thriving.

669
00:35:09,430 --> 00:35:13,100
这些微生物能够在 高砷含量的环境生存
It can cope with hundreds of thousands of times

670
00:35:13,100 --> 00:35:16,170
砷含量高达人类所能承受的
what would be,say,an okay level of arsenic

671
00:35:16,170 --> 00:35:18,970
几千倍
for a human to be exposed to.

672
00:35:18,970 --> 00:35:22,310
弗里曼：这种微生物会是 影子生物圈的一部分吗
(Freeman) Could this microbe be part of the shadow biosphere,

673
00:35:22,310 --> 00:35:25,410
我们自己本土的异形生物
our own homegrown alien life?

674
00:35:25,410 --> 00:35:30,080
它们的生物规则 与我们的完全不同?
Could its rules of biology be different from ours?

675
00:35:30,080 --> 00:35:33,350
那样，也许，可能它们利用 某种类似的DNA
So,perhaps,maybe they use a similar kind of DNA --

676
00:35:33,350 --> 00:35:35,160
只是有点不同
only it's a little different.

677
00:35:35,160 --> 00:35:36,760
也许一部分不同
Maybe parts of it are different.

678
00:35:36,760 --> 00:35:38,860
也许它们利用 和我们类似的蛋白质
Maybe they use similar proteins to ours,

679
00:35:38,860 --> 00:35:40,790
但也许它们利用 与我们不同的氨基酸
but maybe they use different amino acids than we do.

680
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:43,400
弗里曼：如果砷原子
(Freeman) If arsenic atoms

681
00:35:43,400 --> 00:35:47,030
在这些微生物体内替换了磷原子
are somehow replacing phosphorus atoms in these microbes,

682
00:35:47,030 --> 00:35:52,270
那么这些虫子就不符合 我们的进化树
then these bugs do not fit on our tree of life.

683
00:35:52,270 --> 00:35:56,140
它们也许看上去 与我们所知的生命没什么不同
They may not look any different from life as we know it,

684
00:35:56,140 --> 00:35:59,280
但这些虫子也许是完全不同的
but these bugs could be the descendants

685
00:35:59,280 --> 00:36:02,680
另一类基因的后裔
of an entirely separate genesis.

686
00:36:02,680 --> 00:36:05,450
如果我们找到一些
So if we found something

687
00:36:05,450 --> 00:36:07,420
不同的现象
that even did something a little different,

688
00:36:07,420 --> 00:36:09,460
那可能意味着 在地球上
it could mean that here on Earth,

689
00:36:09,460 --> 00:36:11,630
并非只有一棵进化树
there was not just one tree of life,

690
00:36:11,630 --> 00:36:13,690
也许会有很多类型的生命之树
but there could be multiple trees of life.

691
00:36:13,690 --> 00:36:17,000
人类对我们的孤独
Humanity has spent an eternity

692
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:19,570
进行了永恒的思考
thinking about the loneliness of being us.

693
00:36:19,570 --> 00:36:24,770
这会给我们提供一些不同的例子
It would provide for us an example of something else,

694
00:36:24,770 --> 00:36:27,170
某种其他形式的生命 同样成功的生命
some other form of life that was also successful.

695
00:36:28,580 --> 00:36:30,540
Davies:如果我们发现这些微生物
(Davies) If we found those microorganisms,

696
00:36:30,540 --> 00:36:31,910
那就对极了
then bingo.

697
00:36:31,910 --> 00:36:35,320
我们可以说地球上 存在至少两类生命
We could say life on Earth has happened at least twice.

698
00:36:35,320 --> 00:36:38,250
在地球这样的行星上 存在两类生命
Two out of two on one earth-like planet

699
00:36:38,250 --> 00:36:40,790
自然意味着在宇宙中充满了生命
surely means that the universe is teeming with life.

700
00:36:40,790 --> 00:36:42,060
如果是生命在地球这样的环境
It would be inconceivable

701
00:36:42,060 --> 00:36:44,420
能够生成两次，可是在 其他类地行星上却一次没有
life had happened twice on one earth-like planet

702
00:36:44,430 --> 00:36:47,390
那是不可思议的
and not at all on all the other earth-like planets.

703
00:36:47,400 --> 00:36:50,530
弗里曼：如果Felisa的虫子
(Freeman) If Felisa's bugs

704
00:36:50,530 --> 00:36:53,630
是地球上第二类基因的产物
are the offspring of a second genesis here on Earth,

705
00:36:53,630 --> 00:36:56,500
那么生命就可能是宇宙准则
then life could be a cosmic norm.

706
00:36:56,500 --> 00:36:59,610
我们在宇宙中不是孤独的
We would not be alone in the universe.

707
00:36:59,610 --> 00:37:03,580
但这会给我们一个更加 有趣的可能性
But that leads us to an even more intriguing possibility --

708
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:07,550
地球上的生命也许 根本不是源自地球
life on Earth may not be from Earth at all.

709
00:37:15,030 --> 00:37:18,330
科学家探索生命起源的努力
The scientific quest to discover the origin of life

710
00:37:19,140 --> 00:37:21,840
揭露出完全超出想象的情况
has revealed something totally unexpected...

711
00:37:23,580 --> 00:37:26,480
...也许有不止一类基因
...There might have been more than one genesis.

712
00:37:26,480 --> 00:37:32,080
我们的星球上也许存在不止一个 而是2个或者更多的进化树
Our planet might harbor not one,but two or more trees of life,

713
00:37:32,090 --> 00:37:35,220
每一棵都是发自不同的种子
each growing from a separate seed.

714
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:40,330
这些种子来自何处？
Where did these seeds come from?

715
00:37:40,330 --> 00:37:44,260
这个问题使我们重新评价 我们究竟是什么
That question is forcing us to reassess who we really are

716
00:37:44,260 --> 00:37:49,330
因为答案可能在这个世界之外
because the answer could be out of this world.

717
00:37:54,540 --> 00:37:56,540
行星学科学家Ben Weiss
Planetary scientist Ben Weiss

718
00:37:56,540 --> 00:37:59,410
在他的实验室里 有其他世界的碎片
has pieces of another world in his lab.

719
00:37:59,410 --> 00:38:03,080
那是从火星来的陨石
They are rocks that have traveled from Mars to Earth,

720
00:38:03,080 --> 00:38:07,090
他认为火星微生物
and he thinks microscopic Martians

721
00:38:07,090 --> 00:38:10,160
也许就在陨石上搭了便车
may have hitched a ride on some of them.

722
00:38:10,160 --> 00:38:14,260
每年，大概有一吨的 火星陨石落在地球上
About a ton of Martian rocks lands on earth every year,

723
00:38:14,260 --> 00:38:16,400
而在太阳系的历史上
and over the history of the solar system,

724
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:19,000
几十亿吨的材料经过传输
billions of tons of materials have been transferred.

725
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:22,300
所以，实际上 我们有可能就是火星人
So it's possible that we,in fact,are Martians.

726
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:25,240
弗里曼：40亿年前
(Freeman) 4 billion years ago,

727
00:38:25,240 --> 00:38:29,170
地球还在 被彗星和陨石撞击
when Earth was being pounded by meteorites and comets,

728
00:38:29,180 --> 00:38:31,440
火星也是如此
so was Mars.

729
00:38:31,440 --> 00:38:33,580
撞击的碎片
Shrapnel from those impacts

730
00:38:33,580 --> 00:38:37,180
四处飞散，遍布早期太阳系
was flying all over the early solar system.

731
00:38:37,180 --> 00:38:40,490
科学家发现了一块火星岩石
Scientists have found one Martian rock

732
00:38:40,490 --> 00:38:43,760
来自那个充满了行星暴力的时代
that dates back to those days of interplanetary violence.

733
00:38:43,760 --> 00:38:48,130
这块石头名为ALH84001
It's called ALH84001.

734
00:38:48,130 --> 00:38:52,160
ALH84001是一块火星陨石
ALH84001 is a Martian meteorite

735
00:38:52,170 --> 00:38:54,870
在火星表面形成
that formed on the surface of Mars

736
00:38:54,870 --> 00:38:57,170
然后被击出了火星
and then was knocked off the planet.

737
00:38:57,170 --> 00:38:58,740
它在星际漫游
It wandered around in space,

738
00:38:58,740 --> 00:39:01,240
大概1万1千年前在地球着陆
and it landed on the earth about 11,000 years ago.

739
00:39:01,240 --> 00:39:05,140
1984年，一些美国科学家 在南极洲找到了它
It was found in Antarctica by some U.S. scientists in 1984.

740
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:08,580
这块石头很特别
This rock is very special.

741
00:39:10,650 --> 00:39:12,550
弗里曼：1990年代
(Freeman) In the 1990s,

742
00:39:12,550 --> 00:39:15,520
在ALH84001上发现了微小的 蠕虫似的结构
the discovery of tiny,wormlike structures in the rock

743
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:21,090
这使它成为国际焦点
turned ALH84001 into an international celebrity.

744
00:39:21,090 --> 00:39:24,500
有人认为这是火星微生物
The claims that these were fossilized remains

745
00:39:24,500 --> 00:39:28,070
留下的化石痕迹，但受到怀疑
of Martian microbes have since been discredited,

746
00:39:28,070 --> 00:39:31,500
可是Ben对这些微生物的调查
but Ben's investigation of this meteorite

747
00:39:31,500 --> 00:39:34,410
也许仍能提供火星生命的证据
might still offer proof of life on Mars

748
00:39:34,410 --> 00:39:37,740
因为这块岩石被磁化了
because the rock is magnetized.

749
00:39:37,740 --> 00:39:41,750
你看到所有这些小点，小特征
You see all these little dots,little features.

750
00:39:41,750 --> 00:39:44,020
那是在火星上磁化产生的
That's magnetization that originated on Mars.

751
00:39:44,020 --> 00:39:47,220
实际上，那是40亿年前的磁化效应
In fact,it's 4-billion-year-old magnetization.

752
00:39:47,220 --> 00:39:49,820
那就意味着有一个磁场
So that must mean that there was a magnetic field,

753
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:53,190
40亿年甚至更久之前 地球上存在全球磁场
a global magnetic field on Mars 4 billion years ago or earlier.

754
00:39:53,190 --> 00:39:58,860
弗里曼：全球的磁场就像是 一个防护罩
(Freeman) A global magnetic field acts like a protective cocoon.

755
00:39:58,870 --> 00:40:02,770
同样的磁场使 地球的大气得以保持
It has kept Earth's atmosphere safe since day one.

756
00:40:02,770 --> 00:40:04,500
没有磁场
Without a magnetic field,

757
00:40:04,500 --> 00:40:07,840
地球就没有阻隔太阳风的保护
the planet has no protection from the solar wind,

758
00:40:07,840 --> 00:40:10,680
太阳风是从太阳来的粒子流
an intense stream of particles from the sun.

759
00:40:10,680 --> 00:40:13,250
几亿年太阳风的吹拂
Over hundreds of millions of years,

760
00:40:13,250 --> 00:40:17,020
足以把一个行星的大气层吹散
it can blow a planet's atmosphere clean away.

761
00:40:17,020 --> 00:40:22,890
火星现在没有全球磁场 大气层也几乎没有了
Mars has no magnetic field now and almost no atmosphere.

762
00:40:22,890 --> 00:40:27,930
ALH84001中的磁化痕迹证明了
The magnetization imbedded in ALH84001 proves

763
00:40:27,930 --> 00:40:34,100
40亿年前，火星有磁场，有大气
that 4 billion years ago,Mars had both.

764
00:40:34,100 --> 00:40:39,300
我们认为，ALH84001里残存着
ALH84001 contains some trapped,we think,atmospheric gases

765
00:40:39,310 --> 00:40:40,710
早期火星的大气成分
from early Mars.

766
00:40:40,710 --> 00:40:42,910
这些气体的构成
The composition of these gases

767
00:40:42,910 --> 00:40:46,680
与今日火星的大气成分不同
do not resemble the composition of the Martian atmosphere today.

768
00:40:46,680 --> 00:40:48,050
但和我们想象的
But they do resemble

769
00:40:48,050 --> 00:40:52,020
早期火星大气成分差不多
what you might think early Mars had in its atmosphere.

770
00:40:52,020 --> 00:40:54,920
今天，火星又冷又干
Even though today it's cold and dry

771
00:40:54,920 --> 00:40:57,920
表面并不适于生命生存
and not very hospitable for life on the surface,

772
00:40:57,920 --> 00:41:00,960
我们认为在火星的早期
we think in its early days that it had a climate

773
00:41:00,960 --> 00:41:03,130
具有和今日地球差不多的气候
which was much more like the Earth's today.

774
00:41:03,130 --> 00:41:07,600
那时很可能非常温暖湿润
It was presumably significantly warmer and wetter.

775
00:41:07,600 --> 00:41:10,400
甚至在它表面存在有
There might even have been standing bodies of liquid water

776
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:11,770
液态水
on its surface.

777
00:41:11,770 --> 00:41:14,470
它的大气更浓厚
Its atmosphere was thicker.

778
00:41:14,470 --> 00:41:17,210
那也许是一个 更适于生命起源的地方
And it might have been a better place for life to originate.

779
00:41:17,210 --> 00:41:21,580
弗里曼：由于火星只有地球的一半大
(Freeman) Since Mars is only half the size of Earth,

780
00:41:21,580 --> 00:41:24,380
它也许会以比地球快得多的速度
it would have cooled from a molten ball of lava

781
00:41:24,380 --> 00:41:26,480
从一团熔岩的状态冷却下来
much quicker than Earth.

782
00:41:26,490 --> 00:41:32,120
换句话说，火星也许比地球 更早孕育生命
In other words,Mars could have harbored life sooner than earth.

783
00:41:32,120 --> 00:41:36,560
而Ben最近的研究就是关于
And Ben's most recent study is closing the link

784
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:39,300
两个星球生物的联系
between the biology of two planets.

785
00:41:39,300 --> 00:41:40,370
他的发现表明
He's discovering

786
00:41:40,370 --> 00:41:42,830
火星微生物
that microscopic Martians could,indeed,

787
00:41:42,840 --> 00:41:46,910
也许搭乘从火星到地球的陨石列车
have survived the hot and bumpy ride on a space rock

788
00:41:46,910 --> 00:41:49,310
并从高温和颠簸中幸存下来
from Mars to Earth.

789
00:41:49,310 --> 00:41:53,710
那么，你看到的是陨石的一个切片
So,what you see here is a slice of the meteorite.

790
00:41:53,710 --> 00:41:56,680
实际上，在它的外面，在左边
In fact,on the very outside of it over here on the left,

791
00:41:56,680 --> 00:41:58,380
有一点融化的区域
there's a little melted zone.

792
00:41:58,380 --> 00:42:01,120
那是当它穿过地球大气的时候
And that's the zone that got heated to high temperatures

793
00:42:01,120 --> 00:42:03,190
被加热到很高温度的区域
when it passed through Earth's atmosphere.

794
00:42:03,190 --> 00:42:05,660
但是陨石的大部分 在穿越大气时
But most of the meteorite was barely heated at all

795
00:42:05,660 --> 00:42:07,460
并未被加热
by passage through the atmosphere.

796
00:42:07,460 --> 00:42:10,060
弗里曼：由于火星逐渐变冷
(Freeman) As mars was slowly cooling

797
00:42:10,060 --> 00:42:12,900
失去了支撑生命的能力
and losing the ability to support life,

798
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:15,830
最后的火星生命也许跳上船
the last Martians may have jumped ship

799
00:42:15,840 --> 00:42:18,640
来到我们这温暖，湿润的星球
to our warmer,wetter planet.

800
00:42:18,640 --> 00:42:22,010
有机体也许搭上了陨石的便车
Organisms could have hitched a ride on this material

801
00:42:22,010 --> 00:42:24,680
在火星和地球间交换
that was being exchanged between mars and the Earth

802
00:42:24,680 --> 00:42:28,250
甚至给这个着陆的星球 带来了潜在的生命之种
that even potentially seeded the planet on which they landed on.

803
00:42:28,250 --> 00:42:30,720
你知道，有理由这样想
And there's every reason to think that,you know,

804
00:42:30,720 --> 00:42:34,550
如果几十亿年前 在火星上存在生命的起源
if there was an origin of life on Mars billions of years ago,

805
00:42:34,550 --> 00:42:37,560
也许会向地球传播若干次
that it probably made it to the Earth multiple times.

806
00:42:43,930 --> 00:42:46,330
弗里曼：如果Ben是正确的
(Freeman) If Ben is right,

807
00:42:46,330 --> 00:42:49,670
寻找生命起源线索的最佳地点
the best place to look for clues to the origin of life

808
00:42:49,670 --> 00:42:52,940
也许是那已经死亡星球的表面
might be on the surface of planet that's now dead.

809
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:55,910
几十亿年前留下遗迹的 火星原始生命
Fossils of primitive life on Mars

810
00:42:55,910 --> 00:43:00,010
也许还在那里
dating back billions of years could still be there.

811
00:43:00,010 --> 00:43:03,080
也许我们送上火星的 下一个探测器
Perhaps the next space probe we send to Mars

812
00:43:03,080 --> 00:43:04,820
将会在它们头上走过
will stumble across them,

813
00:43:04,820 --> 00:43:09,290
我们就可以研究 长期失散的祖先
and we'll be able to study our long-lost ancestors.

814
00:43:09,290 --> 00:43:13,190
但是，同样有可能 地球上的第一种生物
But it's also possible the first living things on Earth

815
00:43:13,190 --> 00:43:17,060
仍在此地 藏身于影子生物圈
are still here,lurking in the shadow biosphere.

816
00:43:18,630 --> 00:43:21,630
你我是这个上演了 几十亿年故事的
You and I are the latest chapter of a story

817
00:43:21,630 --> 00:43:24,970
最新篇章
that's been unfolding for billions of years.

818
00:43:24,970 --> 00:43:28,370
故事的开头仍旧未知
How that story begins is still unknown.

819
00:43:28,370 --> 00:43:31,640
彗星在地球上播撒了
Did comets seed the Earth

820
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,550
最基本的生命成分?
with the raw ingredients of life?

821
00:43:34,550 --> 00:43:37,150
火星才是我们最初的孕育地？
Was Mars our original birthplace

822
00:43:37,150 --> 00:43:39,880
在我们跳到这个新星球之前？
before we jumped to a new planet?

823
00:43:39,890 --> 00:43:45,260
或者我们是地球的 第二甚至第三类生命？
Or are we the Earth's second or third incarnation of life?

824
00:43:45,260 --> 00:43:47,690
异形在你我间存在
Aliens might be living among us.

825
00:43:47,690 --> 00:43:50,300
我们也许都是火星人
We might all be Martians.

826
00:43:50,300 --> 00:43:53,160
在结尾，科幻小说家可能
In the end,the stuff of science fiction

827
00:43:53,170 --> 00:43:55,630
给我们一个宇宙真理
might lead us to a cosmic truth

828
00:43:55,630 --> 00:43:59,770
回答这个永恒的问题...
and answer that eternal question...

829
00:43:59,770 --> 00:44:01,810
我们从何而来？
How did we get here?

830
00:44:01,940 --> 00:44:05,680
-- 感谢观赏 --

